WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Hearing is access to sound; listening adds attention.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.960
A child may hear a voice without attending to or understanding the message.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.725
Listening age helps describe experience with useful auditory access.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.520
In the lecture's example, consistent waking-hour technology use begins at three months.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:25.190
At the first birthday, that child has about nine months of listening experience, not twelve.

00:00:25.835 --> 00:00:30.235
The calculation is simple, but the interpretation requires care.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:37.560
Device function, signal quality, daily use, and language enrichment all affect what that experience contains.

00:00:38.355 --> 00:00:46.275
Several listening-hours claims on the source slide do not agree arithmetically, so this lab flags them instead of making them answer keys.

00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:51.275
The reliable lesson is that reduced daily access accumulates.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:59.230
Family coaching turns everyday routines into language opportunities, while ongoing audiologic management keeps the input available and useful.

